## 6. Is Assembly Worth Learning? Is It the Fastest Language? Why Doesn't the World Code in It?

Assembly is not a normal language. It is a **human-readable spelling of one CPU's machine code**. Intel x86 assembly and ARM assembly are different languages. There is no single "assembly" that runs everywhere.

### Is it the fastest?

**Closest to the metal ≠ automatically fastest in the real world.**

* In theory, a human can beat a compiler on a **tiny, known** inner loop (crypto, codecs, SIMD).
* In practice, **gcc / clang / LLVM often beat average humans**, because modern CPUs are brutal: out-of-order execution, pipelines, caches, branch prediction, SIMD. Compilers model that. Most people don't.
* "Fastest language" is the wrong question. The CPU only runs machine code. C, Rust, Fortran, and a good JIT all become machine code. **The compiler's output** is what is fast, not the source syntax.
* JS can be *surprisingly* fast on hot typed loops (TurboFan). It still loses to C/Rust on tight number crunching, because of types, GC, and extra checks — not because assembly is magic.

Order of "how close you are to choosing every instruction":

```
hand-written asm  >  C/Rust/Fortran (compiler)  >  Java/C# (JIT)  >  JS (JIT + dynamic)  >  naive interpreter
```

People write **C/Rust + a few asm intrinsics** (`_mm_add_ps`) when they need that last 5%. Almost nobody writes whole apps in asm.

### Why the world doesn't code in it

1. **Not portable** — rewrite for every CPU (x86 laptop, ARM phone, RISC-V). C compiles once per target. JS runs on all of them via the engine.
2. **Too slow to write** — 10 lines of JS ≈ hundreds of asm. A website, a bank, a React app: impossible to ship.
3. **Easy to get wrong** — one wrong register = crash, security hole, silent corruption. No `array.map`, no GC unless you build it.
4. **Compilers won** — for 95% of code, `clang -O2` is faster *and* safer than hand asm.
5. **Hardware moved** — you must retune asm when Intel/Apple change cache and instruction costs. A compiler retunes; your handwritten loop ages.
6. **Teams and features** — products need modules, tests, types, libraries, new people next year. Asm has none of that culture.

We *do* still use it, just in a box: CPU firmware, bootloaders, OS context switch, a 20-line memcpy, game engine inner loop, reverse engineering malware. Hidden under C/Rust.

### Worth learning?

| Goal | Worth it? |
|---|---|
| Frontend / React interviews | **No.** Know that asm ≈ labeled machine code. Stop there. |
| "How languages work" (this file) | **A little.** Registers, stack `push`/`pop`, `mov`, `add`, `call`/`ret`. That's enough to see what V8 and `gcc` emit. |
| Compilers, OS, embedded, reverse engineering, high-freq trading inner loops | **Yes.** |
| "I'll write my app in asm and it will be fastest" | **No.** You'll be slower to ship and often slower to run.

**Interview one-liner:** Assembly is a readable map of *one* CPU's machine code, not a faster universe. The world doesn't write apps in it because it isn't portable and compilers already emit better machine code than most humans. Learn 20 instructions to understand the pipeline; write product code in JS/TS/C/Rust.

**For you (JS interviews):** skip asm as a skill. Keep: CPU runs machine code only; languages differ in runtime (GC, threads, JIT); C/Rust are "fast" because their compilers emit tight native code, not because they bypass the CPU.

