## 7. Raspberry Pi vs Arduino — same rules, different machine

People mix these up. One is a **tiny Linux computer**. The other is a **bare microcontroller**. Both still end as machine code on *a* CPU. The runtime around that CPU is the whole difference.

| | **Arduino** (Uno / Nano class) | **Raspberry Pi** |
|---|---|---|
| What it is | Microcontroller on a board | Credit-card **computer** |
| CPU | Small MCU (AVR, or Cortex-M) | ARM application CPU (like a phone) |
| RAM | **2 KB** on a classic Uno | **1–8 GB** |
| OS | Usually **none** — your sketch *is* the whole program | **Linux** (processes, files, Wi‑Fi, users) |
| Typical language | C / C++ ("Arduino language" is C++) | Python, C, **Node/JS**, even a browser |
| Can it run V8 / React? | No (no RAM, no OS) | Yes |
| Assembly? | Rarely, for a tight blink/timing loop | Same as a laptop: almost never |

### Arduino — why C, not JS, not asm

Your `.ino` is compiled **ahead of time** on your laptop (`avr-gcc`) → machine code → flashed into the chip. When you plug it in, that binary is the only thing running. No browser, no GC, no event loop from V8.

* **~2 KB RAM** — a JS engine cannot fit. One object in Chrome uses more than the whole Uno.
* You talk to **pins** (HIGH/LOW, analogRead). That's memory-mapped hardware, the same idea as "CPU + RAM," plus GPIO.
* World still doesn't write Arduino in assembly: C is already close enough, and the compiler knows AVR. Beginners use `digitalWrite`; the compiler emits the `out`/`sbi` instructions.

This is the "C / malloc / no OS" column from section 5, shrunk to a chip.

### Raspberry Pi — a cheap ARM PC

Pi has a real CPU, real RAM, Linux. **Same model as your laptop**, different chip (ARM, not Intel).

* You can run **Node**, Python, a browser — JS works because there is an OS and hundreds of MB of heap.
* You can *also* bit-bang GPIO like Arduino, but now your JS shares the machine with Linux, Wi‑Fi, and other processes. Timing is sloppier than a bare Arduino.
* Assembly? Still not how you write a Pi app. Same reasons as section 6.

### Which to use (one line)

* **Need a button / LED / sensor, exact timing, tiny power** → Arduino (C on a bare chip).
* **Need a camera, Wi‑Fi server, Python/JS, a screen** → Raspberry Pi (Linux computer).
* **Need both** → Pi talks over USB/serial to an Arduino that handles the pins.

### Does this change "learn assembly?"

A little more **yes** if you go deep embedded (AVR/ARM asm, registers, datasheets). Still **no** for JS interviews. On Arduino you feel "close to the metal" in **C**, not because you wrote `mov`. On Pi you are back in the JS/Python world.

**Interview one-liner:** Arduino is a microcontroller with no OS and kilobytes of RAM — C is compiled to machine code and flashed; JS cannot live there. Raspberry Pi is a small Linux computer — JS runs the same way as on a laptop. Neither is a reason to write product code in assembly.

